' -- The buttons, drawn as the steps the use cases <<include>> -----------------
'  This is the reading that answers "is the system use case just the buttons?":
'  no -- each button is a step INSIDE a use case, so the arrows run from the
'  use case to the button, never the other way round.
usecase "Save role\n(USER / ADMIN)" as S02
usecase "Filter a status, type or reason" as S05
usecase "Filter chips for lists" as S25
usecase "Find (geocode search)" as S29
usecase "Save hub" as S31
usecase "Save achievement" as S36
usecase "Export as PDF" as S43
UC02 ..> S02 : <<include>>
UC05 ..> S05 : <<include>>
UC25 ..> S25 : <<include>>
UC29 ..> S29 : <<include>>
UC31 ..> S31 : <<include>>
UC36 ..> S36 : <<include>>
UC42 ..> S43 : <<include>>

legend bottom right
  Two roles, one boundary:
  - USER  : the Android app only. No use case in this console.
  - ADMIN : everything in the rectangle above.
  --
  Every write needs a typed reason and writes an AdminAction audit row.
  An ADMIN cannot be suspended through the API (manual DB action only).
  Role assignment is the one endpoint that grants a role, with a reason
  (UC-02, PATCH /api/admin/access).
  --
  43 use cases, verb + noun, no "and".
  --
  UC-42/UC-43 are read-only: the report page changes no record and so
  writes no audit row. Export is drawn as a step of viewing the report,
  not as a use case of its own -- it produces a file, it does not
  change the system.
endlegend

@enduml
